const body = `<h1>Documentation with Comments</h1>

<p>
    Comments are for humans. The compiler throws them away. That means a comment
    has exactly one job: change what a human believes about the code. Good
    comments make beliefs accurate. Bad comments make beliefs wrong, which is
    worse than no comments at all.
</p>

<h2>The Three Flavors</h2>

<pre><code>// Line comment — one line. Quick asides, short clarifications.

/* Block comment — spans lines. Explanations that need room.
   You will rarely need this form. */

/**
 * Javadoc — machine-readable documentation. Starts /**
 * Each line begins with a star. Editors read this and
 * show it on hover. Tools turn it into web pages.
 */
public class Example { }</code></pre>

<h2>Javadoc Anatomy</h2>

<pre><code>/**
 * Rolls a single die with the given number of sides.
 * @param sides the number of sides on the die (must be 2 or more)
 * @return a random int between 1 and sides, inclusive
 */
public static int roll(int sides) { ... }</code></pre>

<table>
    <tr><th>Line</th><th>Job</th></tr>
    <tr><td>First sentence(s)</td><td>What the method does. Behavior, not
        implementation. "Returns the higher roll" not "calls roll twice."</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>@param name description</td><td>One per parameter. What it means,
        any constraints.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>@return description</code></td><td>What comes back. If the method
        returns void, no @return.</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="callout warn">
    <strong>One bug in the table above.</strong> The @param row is missing its
    closing <code>&lt;/code&gt;</code> tag. Did you catch it before the note told
    you? A reader who catches documentation bugs is a reader who is actually
    reading. Yes, that was planted on purpose.
</div>

<h2>What Comments Are For</h2>

<table>
    <tr><th>Good comment</th><th>Bad comment</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>// exclusive upper bound; index "to" itself is NOT included</code></td>
        <td><code>// set x to x plus 1</code> (repeats the code, adds nothing)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>// gold is stored in copper; divide by 100 for display</code> (a
        fact you could not guess)</td>
        <td><code>// increment gold</code> (says what, not why)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>// TODO: balance test, dragon gold seems too low</code></td>
        <td><code>// this method works</code> (a claim, not information)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>// Workaround: Scanner has no hasNextInt in our scope, so we
        catch instead</code> (records a decision)</td>
        <td>A comment that contradicts the code (a lie)</td></tr>
</table>

<p>The pattern: good comments carry WHY and CONSTRAINTS, the things code cannot
express. Bad comments translate the code into English, which the code already
does, being code.</p>

<h2>Lying Comments Bug Hunt</h2>

<div class="bug-hunt">
    <h3>Find the Lie in Each Pair</h3>
    <p>In each snippet the comment and the code disagree. The comment is the
    suspect; decide whether to fix the comment or the code:</p>
    <pre><code>// returns a random number from 1 to sides
public static int roll(int sides) {
    return (int)(Math.random() * sides);
}

// prints the player's name and gold
System.out.println(name + " has " + gold + " HP");

// halves the stack, rounding up
stack /= 2;

// true if score beats the high score
return score &gt;= highScore;</code></pre>
    <p>Answers: (1) the code returns 0 to sides-1, the comment says 1 to sides.
    Fix the code: add + 1. The comment described intent. (2) The comment says gold,
    the code prints HP. One of them is wrong about reality, and only the design
    doc knows which. That ambiguity, in real code, is how bugs live for years.
    (3) Integer division truncates, so 5 / 2 is 2, which rounds DOWN. Fix the
    comment. (4) "&gt;=" means ties count as beating. Is a tie a new record? Arcade
    tradition says ties do NOT count, so the comment is the intent and the code
    should be &gt;. But you could argue the other way. The point: precision in
    words forces precision in code.</p>
</div>

<h2>Tinker Challenge: Publish Your API</h2>

<div class="tinker">
    <h3>Document Dice.java Completely</h3>
    <ol>
        <li>Javadoc every public method in your Dice.java: first sentence,
        @param, @return.</li>
        <li>Hover-test: call each method from a tester and hover the call. Your
        documentation should pop up, exactly like Math.random does.</li>
        <li>Trade files with a partner. Their job: find one method whose javadoc
        is incomplete or wrong. Report it politely, like a coworker, not like a
        grader.</li>
        <li>Class-level javadoc on Dice.java itself: what is this class, who uses
        it, what does it never do (no printing? no Scanner?).</li>
    </ol>
</div>

<h2>Socratic Checkpoint</h2>

<div class="socratic">
    <h3>Why is Practice 4 of the exam about documentation?</h3>
    <p>Because code is read far more often than it is written, and usually by
    people who were not there when it was written. Documenting is engineering for
    the reader. 10-15% of the multiple-choice section tests whether you can
    describe behavior and conditions precisely. That is the comment-writing skill,
    tested without comments.</p>
    <h3>When is a comment a smell?</h3>
    <p>When you need a comment to explain WHAT code does, the code is usually too
    clever and should be rewritten to be clearer. Comments should explain WHY and
    record CONSTRAINTS. If you are writing "what" comments, try renaming variables
    and methods instead, and see if the comment becomes unnecessary.</p>
</div>

